  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Nevada federal contract obligations totaled $2.9B in FY2026 to date. The number of awards reached 18,828 with an average award size of $152K. Obligations are down 29.0% year over year. The Department of Defense obligated $1.1B and the Department of Energy obligated $1B through FY2026 to date. MISSION SUPPORT & TEST SERVICES LLC received $953M and FLEET VEHICLE SOURCE INC received $427M in obligations. Facilities Support Services accounted for $1B in industry obligations, followed by Engineering Services at $556M and Automobile Manufacturing at $427M. Fiscal-year obligations were $3.1B in FY21, $3.3B in FY22, $3.7B in FY23, $4B in FY24, $4B in FY25, and $2.9B in FY26. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). This snapshot shows concentration by a few agencies, a small group of large vendors, and a mix of facilities, engineering, and vehicle-related industries driving obligations in Nevada.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why are FY2026 to date obligations lower than prior years?",
        "body": "Fiscal-year obligations by fiscal year show $4B in FY24 and $4B in FY25, compared with $2.9B in FY26 to date. Obligations are down 29.0% year over year, reflecting lower recorded obligations so far in the current fiscal year. The current-year total is partial, fiscal year to date, which limits direct year-end comparisons."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate Nevada federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $1.1B in obligations, followed by the Department of Energy with $1B and the General Services Administration with $493M. MISSION SUPPORT & TEST SERVICES LLC is the largest vendor with $953M, followed by FLEET VEHICLE SOURCE INC at $427M and JT4 LLC at $330M. Facilities Support Services leads industries with $1B, and Engineering Services follows at $556M."
      }
    ]
  },
